<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/intermediate/machine-learning-in-one-picture">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Artificial Intelligence, built directly on the
    foundational lesson <em>How Machines Learn</em>: you should already know what a model, a
    dataset and a prediction are, and why learning from data differs from hand-written
    rules. No coding is needed; everything runs on ideas and worked examples.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the four learning paradigms and say what signal each one learns from;</li>
    <li>state Tom Mitchell's definition of learning and use it to check whether a system is really learning;</li>
    <li>decide, for a given problem, whether supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement or self-supervised learning fits;</li>
    <li>read a train-versus-test gap as evidence of overfitting;</li>
    <li>predict how each paradigm characteristically fails, and what evaluation discipline guards against it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The AI field organises itself into four learning paradigms, defined by a single
    question: <strong>where does the learning signal come from?</strong> Labelled
    examples (supervised), raw structure (unsupervised), reward from actions
    (reinforcement), or the data itself (self-supervised). Everything from spam
    filters to ChatGPT fits one of these boxes — and knowing which box tells you
    immediately what data you need, what failure modes to expect, and what the
    result will and will not be able to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    One definition anchors everything. Tom Mitchell's (1997): a program
    <strong>learns from experience E with respect to task T and performance measure P
    if its performance at T, measured by P, improves with E</strong>. The definition is
    deliberately unromantic — no consciousness, just measurable improvement from
    exposure — and it is what makes machine learning an engineering discipline: you
    can state the task, pick the signal, and measure whether the experience helped.
    This lesson walks the four sources of signal, then the decision framework for
    choosing between them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four paradigms, defined by their learning signal" widget="equationStep" config={mlpicCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards and read each one as an
    answer to the same question: where does the learning signal come from? Labels supplied
    by humans (supervised), structure discovered in raw data (unsupervised), reward returned
    by an environment (reinforcement), labels manufactured from the data itself
    (self-supervised). Try sorting a system you use daily — a spam filter, a photo
    grouper — into one of the four boxes.
  </p>

  <h2>Supervised: learning from labelled examples</h2>
  <p>
    The workhorse of applied ML. You supply examples with answers — emails marked
    spam/not-spam, houses with sold prices, X-rays with diagnoses — and an algorithm
    finds the mapping from inputs to outputs. Two sub-species by output type:
    <strong>classification</strong> (discrete labels: spam or not, cat or dog) and
    <strong>regression</strong> (continuous values: a price, a temperature). The
    machinery: a model (a family of functions with adjustable parameters), a loss
    function measuring wrongness on the examples, and an optimiser adjusting
    parameters to reduce the loss (the neural-networks lesson builds this loop by
    hand). The critical concept is <strong>generalisation</strong>: the goal is not
    performance on the training examples but performance on <em>new</em> ones — which
    is why data is split into train and test sets, and why memorising the training
    data (overfitting) is the field's central villain. A worked shape: 10,000
    labelled loans, 8,000 for training, 2,000 held out; a model that achieves 95%
    accuracy on its own training data and 71% on the held-out set has learned the
    training set, not lending — the gap between the two numbers is the most
    important number in applied ML.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the model scored 95% on its training data, so it is a
  good model." Training performance is the easiest score to inflate: a model with enough
  capacity can memorise its examples without learning anything that transfers. The only
  number that matters is the one on data the model never saw — which is why the held-out
  set is sacred.</p>
  <p>
    Where the labels come from is half the engineering: human annotation (expensive,
    slow, biased by whoever paid for it), historical records (free, but they encode
    the past's decisions and biases — the model learns last year's lending policy,
    not the truth), or logged behaviour (clicks as labels — which measure what users
    did, not what was right). The supervised paradigm's whole character — its power
    and its pathologies — flows from this: <strong>the model can only be as good as
    the signal in the labels</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> supervised learning turns labelled examples into a mapping
    from inputs to outputs, and its character is set by the quality of the labels. Next,
    what can be learned when nobody supplies answers at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Unsupervised: finding structure without labels</h2>
  <p>
    No answers supplied — just data, and the question "what structure is in here?".
    The three canonical jobs. <strong>Clustering</strong> (k-means is the classic:
    pick k centroids, assign each point to its nearest, move each centroid to its
    cluster's mean, repeat until still — watch it converge on customer data into
    spend-frequency segments); <strong>dimensionality reduction</strong> (PCA finds
    the directions of greatest variance, so 200 correlated features become 10
    informative axes — compression that makes later modelling possible and reveals
    what actually varies); <strong>anomaly detection</strong> (model what "normal"
    looks like, flag what doesn't — fraud, faults, intrusions). The epistemological
    caution from the widget matters most here: the algorithm defines similarity
    itself (k-means measures distance; it will happily cluster by height when you
    meant spending), so clusters are <em>suggestions</em> requiring human
    interpretation — and k will always find k clusters whatever you feed it. The
    validation is practical: do the segments predict different behaviour? Do the
    anomalies, checked by hand, turn out to be real? Unsupervised structure earns
    trust only by usefulness.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "clustering finds the groups that are really in the
  data." It finds the groups its distance measure defines: k-means will happily partition
  customers by height when you meant spending, and it always returns k clusters whatever
  you feed it. The structure is real arithmetic; whether it means anything is a human
  judgment, validated by usefulness.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> supervised learning fits a mapping to labelled examples;
    unsupervised learning proposes structure in raw data and leaves the interpretation
    to you. The third paradigm changes the question entirely: there, the machine acts
    first and learns from what happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Reinforcement: learning from reward</h2>
  <p>
    The paradigm of <strong>sequential decisions</strong>: an agent acts in an
    environment, the environment returns reward (win, score, profit), and the agent
    adjusts its policy — its action-choosing rule — toward more reward. The
    distinctive difficulty is <strong>credit assignment</strong>: a chess move's
    value shows up fifty moves later, so which of the fifty actions earned the
    reward? And its second difficulty is the <strong>exploration–exploitation
    dilemma</strong>: the agent must try suboptimal actions to discover better ones
    while simultaneously cashing in on what it already knows. The triumph that
    recast the field: AlphaGo (2016) beat Lee Sedol 4–1 — move 37 of game 2, a
    shoulder hit so alien that commentators first called it a mistake, then realised
    it was play no human would have found, from a system that had trained against
    itself. The costs are symmetrical: reinforcement learning is sample-hungry
    (millions of games are cheap in Go, expensive in robotics), and reward functions
    are dangerous to specify — the agent optimises <em>the reward you wrote</em>,
    not the intention you had (the specification problem that safety research
    exists for). Modern LLM tuning (RLHF — reinforcement learning from human
    feedback) brought the paradigm into the mainstream: humans rank responses, a
    reward model learns the ranking, and the language model is nudged toward it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the agent that beat the world champion must have
  understood Go the way a human player does." It optimised a reward signal against itself
  over millions of games. The triumph and the danger are the same fact: the system
  optimises the reward it was given, not the understanding you imagined.</p>

  <h2>Self-supervised: the label generator</h2>
  <p>
    The bridge that made the modern era possible, and the quiet idea behind it:
    <strong>labels can be manufactured from the data itself</strong>. Mask a word and
    ask the model to predict it (the answer is in the sentence); show half an image
    and ask for the other half; jumble a sentence and ask for the order. No human
    annotation — the data is its own teacher — so the scale bottleneck (labelling
    costs money; the internet is free) disappears. The recipe that now dominates:
    <strong>pretrain</strong> self-supervised on enormous raw corpora (learning
    language, images, audio as such), then <strong>fine-tune</strong> on small
    supervised sets for specific tasks (or align with human feedback). Every
    foundation model — the LLMs, the image encoders behind modern vision systems —
    is this two-step. Notice what it changes conceptually: the paradigm is
    supervised learning with an automated label factory, which is why it inherits
    supervised learning's mechanics (loss, generalisation, overfitting) at a scale
    where the old intuitions about data volume stop applying.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "large language models learn without any supervision,
  so they are unsupervised learning." They are self-supervised: supervision is there,
  just manufactured from the data itself — mask a word, predict it, check the answer
  against the text. That makes them supervised learners at heart, overfitting included,
  which is why held-out evaluation never stops mattering.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four sources of signal, four paradigms — labels, structure,
    reward, and labels made from the data itself. Next, a decision procedure for choosing
    among them.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a paradigm by problem shape</h2>
  <p>
    The decision framework, as questions. <strong>Have labelled history?</strong>
    Supervised — churn prediction, fraud, pricing, diagnosis; start here, it covers
    most business ML. <strong>No labels, want structure?</strong> Unsupervised —
    segmentation, grouping, anomaly flagging; budget human time for interpreting
    what comes out. <strong>Sequential decisions with feedback and a simulator or
    live environment?</strong> Reinforcement — control, game-playing, optimising a
    recommender; budget for fragility. <strong>Massive raw data, few labels, and a
    downstream task?</strong> Self-supervised pretrain then fine-tune — or, the
    increasingly common answer, <em>use someone else's pretrained foundation
    model</em> and spend your labelled budget on fine-tuning. Two honest caveats
    close the framework: real systems are hybrids (a self-supervised backbone with
    a supervised head, tuned with reinforcement from human preferences — the LLM
    stack is all four paradigms in one artefact), and the paradigm choice does not
    excuse the data-quality discipline of the data-science strand — every paradigm
    eats the dataset it is given.
  </p>

  <h2>The vocabulary that spans all four</h2>
  <p>
    Learn these once and every ML conversation becomes readable. <strong>Features</strong>:
    the input variables fed to the model (the columns). <strong>Model</strong>: the
    parameterised function being fitted. <strong>Training</strong>: adjusting
    parameters to reduce loss on examples. <strong>Loss</strong>: the wrongness
    score. <strong>Generalisation</strong>: performance on unseen data — the actual
    objective. <strong>Overfitting/underfitting</strong>: memorising noise versus
    missing signal — diagnosable by the train-versus-test gap (overfit: great
    train, poor test; underfit: poor both). <strong>Train/validation/test</strong>:
    the three-way split — fit on train, tune choices on validation, report once on
    test (touching test repeatedly is how test sets quietly become training data —
    the reproducibility sin of the field). And <strong>inference</strong>: the
    trained model used in production, where latency, cost and drift (the world
    changing under a frozen model) become the engineering problems. With that
    vocabulary, the neural-networks lesson's backpropagation will read as one
    particular — and spectacularly successful — way of doing the training step.
  </p>

  <h2>How the paradigms fail: the failure mode map</h2>
  <p>
    Each paradigm fails characteristically, and knowing the signature is half of
    operations. <strong>Supervised</strong> fails through its labels: label noise
    caps accuracy, historical bias replicates history (the model learns last
    year's discrimination as this year's pattern), and <strong>leakage</strong> — a
    feature that encodes the answer indirectly (the "cancelled" flag that only
    exists after churn) — produces spectacular test scores that evaporate in
    production. <strong>Unsupervised</strong> fails through interpretation: the
    structure is real arithmetic but possibly meaningless — clusters that mirror
    the data collection process rather than the world (two sensors, two
    collection eras), and k chosen because the slide needs three segments.
    <strong>Reinforcement</strong> fails through specification: reward hacking
    (the agent exploits the metric — the boat-racing agent that looped in circles
    to farm waypoints rather than finishing the race), and sim-to-real gaps where
    policies trained in simulation meet physics. <strong>Self-supervised</strong>
    fails through its shortcuts: the model learns the easiest predictable
    feature of the masking task, which may not be the understanding you wanted
    (image models predicting from watermark position rather than content). The
    map's lesson is uniform: <strong>every paradigm optimises the signal you gave
    it, not the intention you had</strong> — which is why evaluation discipline
    (held-out data, human checks, production monitoring) is the permanent layer
    above all four boxes.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State Mitchell's definition of learning: what are T, E and P?</li>
    <li>Predicting a house price from features versus grouping customers with no target variable: which paradigm is each, and what decides it?</li>
    <li>A model scores 96% on its training data and 70% on held-out data. What is the diagnosis, and what would you do?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A program learns from experience E with respect to task
    T and performance measure P if its performance at T, measured by P, improves with E.
    (2) The price prediction
    is supervised regression (a numeric target); the grouping is unsupervised clustering
    (no labels). The learning signal, not the algorithm, decides the box. (3) Overfitting:
    the model memorised the training set. Simplify the model or improve the signal, and
    trust only the held-out number.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The four paradigms are defined by one question: where does the learning signal come from?</li>
    <li>Supervised learning fits mappings to labelled examples; generalisation to unseen data is the objective.</li>
    <li>Unsupervised learning proposes structure; clusters are suggestions to validate, not truths discovered.</li>
    <li>Reinforcement learning optimises the reward you wrote — sample-hungry and fragile to misspecification.</li>
    <li>Self-supervised learning manufactures labels from raw data, enabling the pretrain-then-fine-tune recipe.</li>
    <li>The train-versus-test gap is the field's most important number: a big gap means overfitting.</li>
    <li>Every paradigm optimises the signal it was given, not the intention you had — evaluation is permanent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lessons fill in the boxes: <em>Classification and
    Regression</em> trains a supervised model properly, <em>Image Recognition (CNNs)</em>
    applies it to vision, and <em>Neural Networks from Scratch</em> builds the training
    loop by hand. The advanced strand then takes up <em>Reinforcement Learning</em>,
    <em>Transformers and Large Language Models</em> and <em>Evaluation, Bias and
    Safety</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four notes from the expert shelf. <strong>No free lunch</strong>: averaged over
    all possible problems, no learning algorithm beats any other — every
    algorithm's power comes from assumptions about the world (its
    <em>inductive bias</em>: convolutions assume locality; recurrence assumes
    sequence; linear models assume linearity), which is why matching the model's
    bias to the problem's structure matters more than model choice among
    reasonable candidates. <strong>Data-centric AI</strong>: the recent corrective
    to model obsession — for fixed compute, cleaning and rebalancing 1,000 labels
    often beats upgrading the architecture, because the label noise caps the
    achievable accuracy (a model cannot learn signal the labels do not contain).
    <strong>Where the paradigms blur</strong>: semi-supervised (few labels, many
    unlabelled examples), weak supervision (noisy programmatic labels),
    imitation learning (demonstrations as reward-shaped supervision) — the
    taxonomy is a map of the learning <em>signal</em>, and hybrid signals are the
    norm at the frontier. <strong>Evaluation is the discipline that survives
    every paradigm</strong>: whatever the signal, the questions stay the same —
    measured on what population, against what baseline, with what leakage
    risks — and the lessons that drilled them (the data-science strand) apply
    unchanged to a 500-billion-parameter model. Hold onto the map in this lesson
    and every model you meet — from a spreadsheet-sized regression to a frontier
    LLM — arrives pre-explained: ask what the signal is, and the paradigm
    announces itself.
  </p>
